inner monologue is about sort of all the  things that I feel and think about on a  daily basis it talks about love you know  heartbreak and anxiety and depression  and a lot of things that I feel like  people some people are like super  comfortable with hearing about yet and  that kind of stuff but I really wanted  it to be as true to me as possible  yeah and and it's become really special  you know I really think you like attract  like-minded people so every time we do a  show and they're all singing with me  it's like okay this is this is exactly  what I wanted and why I did this you  know the people are out there but were  you a little bit shocked just how  relatable those themes were when people  when you started hearing from people  where you started seeing the numbers of  like-minded people who were going  through the same thing what was that  like totally it was overwhelming  sometimes I do like meet and greets and  I'll get some really intense stories  yeah but for the most part it's it's  beautiful to know that you know none of  us are really alone even when we think  we are yeah like we have way more in  common than we all think we do were  there people trying to steer you away  from being that open on the on the  record or know you very much call the  shots  ya know what if they were I would have  told them to f off perfect the  relationship with you and Selena is  awesome on record what is it like in  real life it's it's it's the same yeah  same as you would think I mean I love  her to death I I think she's so strong  and she's just so beautiful and humble  and I'm grateful that I even get to be  around her  yeah learned from her and she's gone  through a lot and when you are sort of  that open with fans whether it's Selena  with what she went through or you  talking about the themes on the album  does that make a connection with the two  of you sitting down to do a song  together even better when you both have  sort of super young but have gone  through so much well I can't speak for  her but I can say that you know  perspective is everything yeah  and I think there's a lot of  contributing factors that go into  writing a song I think I think probably  for me more so it's personal experience  yeah than it is about people around me  yeah at first I think it starts as me  and then it becomes about the people  around me  do you write better when you're in a  dark place Oh see I don't I don't know I  think it depends on my mood like I read  it I write a majority of stuff when I'm  feeling upset but if I'm in love with  somebody or something then I'm like like  I was super in love when I made any  monologue and I wrote a song code deep  under this uncut Apple that song in a  Morlock when I was super in love yeah  and it was just like two of those songs  that just like flowed right out of me so  and that love's not around anymore  oh no no okay so how what's your  relationship like with those songs that  you write when you're super in love  afterwards they feel a little bitter but  but I find that as you as you the more  you do this and the more life you live  the the more you have things to  associate them with other than so it may  it may be one thing when you write it  but it could take on a completely other  form later down the line knowing when  you're on tour and you're touring with  pink that we all know what a pink show  looks like so what what does that do for  you knowing that you have to hit that  stage first knowing what is to come you  know to be honest what her and I do are  completely different I like that I get  to just like you know warm up the crowd  for her yeah and and then just to see  everyone's face when she's twirling  around the arena like in six-inch heels  all the way around and people just like  don't know what to expect and it's fun I  like I like I like being on the stage  but I also like getting off because the  faster I get off that means the faster  she gets on and I get to watch it -  you've opened for some really incredible  acts what has that experience sort of  what have you taken away individually  from all the big artists that you've  opened  for that you then bring on your own  shows when you're when you're headlining  is it an attitude is that how you treat  people is definitely how you treat  people  yeah for sure I've been I've been lucky  enough to be an opener and open with a  lot of my friends and I've heard horror  stories about how some people treat  their openers and how they treat people  and yeah I just don't ever want to like  run my ship like that ever no so yeah I  think if anything it's taught me to like  just be appreciative of all the things  that we get to do and even though it can  be kind of mentally draining you know  when I like just sleep for 82 hours you  know the bigger picture is you know you  get to do what you love every day yeah  what do you do live when it's it's  either a sound that you did with Selena  or with Nile or what do you do live with  those songs when you have most of the  time I just sing them yeah just as this  or or I'll have the audience the crowd  see seeing their parts but yeah most of  the times just me yeah your relationship  with Niall how's that that's good yeah  yeah he's a sweetheart what do you got  what do you where did you guys connect  where what son sort of what level did  did you guys find common ground well we  actually met at them much Music Awards  oh you did yeah in Canada and that was  when we first initially met and then he  asked me to go on tour with him last  spring yeah so we were around each other  for like three months straight mm-hmm so  we've just become good friends from that  and when I wrote what a time I knew I  wanted to have a male's perspective on  it and I've just always loved niles  voice and I just love him as a person  and I sent it to him and was like if you  like this this could be fun you just  come like knocking here like with the  black with pink how did